{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>And not with you alone.</b> That is, it is with this generation that I make the covenant and the oath, for you are the ones standing with us this day before HaShem our Elohim — but even those who are not here with us this day, that is, in this covenant. And he said so because the root is the father and the children are the branches destined to come forth from the power of the root; therefore the root was able to bring future generations under the oath. And our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted that all the souls created from the beginning were there, though the body had not yet been created. According to this, the interpretation of the verse will be: 'and those who are not here' — in body — 'with us this day' — in soul. And the word 'with us' recurs back to the word 'standing,' as if it said, 'and those who are not here standing with us this day.' And he used of them the term 'standing,' in the language spoken of the ministering angels (Zechariah 3:7): ״בין העומדים האלה״. And so our Rabbis of blessed memory said that all the prophets, all of them, received their prophecy at Sinai, but they were not given permission to prophesy until their own time. This is what they said in Midrash Tanchuma on the verse (Malachi 1:1): ״משא דבר ה' אל ישראל ביד מלאכי״ — it was not said ״על מלאכי״ but ״ביד מלאכי״, because the prophecy was already in his hand from Mount Sinai. And so Isaiah of blessed memory said (Isaiah 48:16): ״לא מראש בסתר דברתי מעת היותה שם אני ועתה ה' אלהים שלחני ורוחו״, explaining that from Mount Sinai he received the prophecy, but it was not given to him permission to be sent until now."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 29, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:51:53.675676518Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}